This PR improves documentation across three core classes in the PetClinic application as part of the sustainable coding practices evaluation for SE754 Assignment 5.

## Problem
Manual review and SonarQube feedback showed that several important classes in the owner and vet areas were under-documented. While the code was functional, the intent of the domain objects and controller flows was not immediately clear to readers, especially around model preparation, pet ownership rules, and veterinarian specialty handling.

The `Owner` domain model, `PetController`, and `Vet` model each contain logic that benefits from clearer class-level and method-level documentation. Without that context, future contributors have to infer behavior from implementation details rather than from the API contract.

## Changes
- Expanded the class-level documentation in `Owner.java` to explain its role as the core owner entity and its relationship to pets
- Added method-level documentation in `PetController.java` to clarify:
  - model attribute preparation
  - request binding
  - validation flow
  - pet create/update handling
- Improved the class and method documentation in `Vet.java` to explain:
  - specialty management
  - the read-only sorted view exposed to callers

## Sustainability Impact
Better documentation reduces cognitive load for future maintenance work. These classes are part of the main application flow, so clearer comments and Javadoc help new contributors understand why the code exists, not just what it does.

This improves long-term maintainability by:
- reducing onboarding friction for new contributors
- making controller and domain behavior easier to reason about
- lowering the risk of incorrect future modifications
- improving overall code readability and maintainability

## Files Changed
- `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/owner/Owner.java`
- `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/owner/PetController.java`
- `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/vet/Vet.java`"}, {"evidence_id": "rebuild-20261014:f182358d02e4a68e52bdbabf55ca7800288511e7:spring-projects/spring-petclinic:pullrequest:2573", "kind": "PullRequest", "path": null, "commit": null, "number": 2573, "title": "Prevent duplicate pet creation with similar name for the same owner under concurrent requests", "email": null, "login": null, "name": null, "character_offset": 0, "excerpt": "## Summary
This Pull Request resolves a concurrent race condition in the Spring PetClinic application that allows users to create duplicate pets with the same name (case-insensitive) under the same owner.

## Problem
In a concurrent environment, if two requests are sent simultaneously to create a pet with the same name for the same owner, both checks succeed, bypassing the in-memory validation check and inserting duplicate pet records.

## Steps to Reproduce

1. Start the Spring Boot application locally.
2. Create an owner (or use an existing owner with no pet named `PRTESTPET`).
3. Send two concurrent `POST` requests to `/owners/{ownerId}/pets/new`, both using the pet name `PRTESTPET`.
4. Open the owner's details page.
5. Observe that both requests succeed and duplicate pets with the same name are created for the same owner.

<img width="728" height="504" alt="Screenshot 2026-07-14 at 1 47 19 AM" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/3c83859f-9660-4030-a583-f0e6953e273f" />


## Root Cause
The duplication validation is executed entirely in Java memory (using a check-then-act pattern: `owner.getPet(petName, false) != null`), and there is no database-level unique constraint or lock to serialize concurrent insert operations.

## Solution
1. **Database-Level Case-Insensitive Uniqueness Constraints**:
   - **H2**: Change the `pets.name` column type to `VARCHAR_IGNORECASE(30)` and add a unique constraint on `(owner_id, name)`.
   - **MySQL**: Add a unique constraint `UNIQUE (owner_id, name)`.
   - **PostgreSQL**: Add a unique functional index: `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX unique_owner_pet_name ON pets (owner_id, LOWER(name));`.
2. **Immediate Persistence**:
   - Change `save()` to `saveAndFlush()` in [PetController.java](file:///Users/spring-petclinic/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/owner/PetController.java) to ensure SQL execution and trigger constraint violations within the try-catch block.
3. **Graceful Exception Translation**:
   - Update [PetController.java](file:///Users/spring-petclinic/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/owner/PetController.java) to catch `DataIntegrityViolationException`, verify if it is the specific `unique_owner_pet_name` violation, and translate it into a clean validation error on the name field.

NOTE: To preserve the existing application behavior, the database const"}]

